# Probability Arena — CRYPTO-COVERAGE-REPAIR-002 sparse horizon observation
# timer (USER unit). NOT auto-installed. Install:
#   cp infra/systemd/user/probability-arena-crypto-sparse-observe.{service,timer} \
#      ~/.config/systemd/user/
#   systemctl --user daemon-reload
#   systemctl --user enable --now probability-arena-crypto-sparse-observe.timer
# Inspect:
#   systemctl --user list-timers | grep crypto-sparse-observe
#   journalctl --user -u probability-arena-crypto-sparse-observe.service -n 50 --no-pager
#
# THIS FILE IS A REPO TEMPLATE. Nothing here is installed on EVO-X2 by
# GATE7-SPARSE-UNITS-001; installation and activation are operator actions, in
# the order documented under "Sparse observer timer" in docs/EVO_X2_RUNBOOK.md
# (which leads with the DISABLE procedure, on purpose). The pass is a clean
# no-op while `enable_crypto_sparse_observation` is off — see the SAFE TO
# INSTALL DARK note in the .service file — so the timer is safe to install dark
# and enable later.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE CADENCE COUPLING — the weakest joint in this lane, and how this file
# stays on the right side of it
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# `SPARSE_CADENCE_MINUTES = 60.0` (app/services/crypto_sparse_observation.py)
# is the period the CODE assumes. Invariant (2) MISSED-PASS TOLERANCE is stated
# against exactly that number, and NOTHING IN THE CODE CAN ENFORCE A systemd
# `OnCalendar=` IT CANNOT SEE. The milestone doc names this the weakest joint in
# the design, and it is right.
#
# THE `OnCalendar` BELOW IS NOT HAND-PICKED. It is what
# `crypto_sparse_observation.timer_oncalendar()` returns for the shipped cadence
# AND the shipped phase (`SPARSE_TIMER_PHASE_MINUTE = 37`, which is why the line
# is not `hourly`) — the same string `crypto-observation-coverage-report` prints as
# `expected timer line: OnCalendar=...` for an operator to COPY rather than
# type. `tests/test_gate7_sparse_units_001.py` parses this file and asserts the
# value equals that function's return, so a change to `SPARSE_CADENCE_MINUTES`
# that does not reach this file fails the suite rather than silently
# de-synchronising the schedule from the invariant.
#
# The mitigations that already shipped stay the operational half of this: the
# report's `previous_write_age_minutes` with `cadence_warning` above 1.5x
# cadence (a WRITE proxy, not a pass heartbeat — read its
# `cadence_warning_means`), and `scheduling_miss_rate`, which is what a slow or
# stopped timer actually looks like one band later (>=7h at 6h, >=25h at 24h).

[Unit]
Description=Run Probability Arena crypto sparse horizon observation hourly at :37

[Timer]
# = timer_oncalendar() at SPARSE_CADENCE_MINUTES = 60 and
# SPARSE_TIMER_PHASE_MINUTE = 37. Still derived, still copy-able from
# `crypto-observation-coverage-report`, still pinned by test against BOTH
# constants — the phase is now part of the derivation rather than a property of
# the `hourly` alias.
#
# THE PHASE IS OFF :00, AND THAT IS WHAT THIS OFFSET BUYS. `hourly` expands to
# `*-*-* *:00:00`, and the :00 instant is occupied on this host:
#   * probability-arena-baseline.timer — `OnCalendar=*-*-* 00/4:00:00`, so
#     :00:00 six times a day (RandomizedDelaySec=300, i.e. :00-:05).
#   * probability-arena-retention.timer — `OnCalendar=daily` (= `*-*-* 00:00:00`),
#     once a day, and it is the DELETE-heavy writer on this host
#     (RandomizedDelaySec=600, i.e. 00:00-00:10).
# THOSE TWO ARE WHAT :37 DODGES. It also stays clear of the reconciler at
# :07-:12 and of the boot-anchored hourly tick-aggregation slot this host's
# uptime put at :22-:24.
#
# ONE ACCEPTED RESIDUAL, STATED RATHER THAN GLOSSED:
# probability-arena-backup.timer
# is `OnCalendar=*-*-* 01:30:00` with `RandomizedDelaySec=600`, so its
# start is uniform over 01:30:00-01:40:00 and CAN land on :37. It is accepted,
# not avoided, and the reasoning is that it is strictly smaller than what the
# offset bought:
#   * ONCE A DAY AT MOST, and only in the 01:xx hour, because that unit's hour
#     field is pinned to 01 — against `baseline`'s six-a-day and `retention`'s
#     once-a-day at an instant this lane could never be jittered away from.
#   * PROBABILISTIC, not deterministic: the overlap depends on where in its
#     600 s jitter the backup lands, so it is not a standing appointment.
#   * The backup is a bounded file copy (SQLITE-BACKUP-COORDINATION-001:
#     measured 37 s, 0 restarts, lock events unchanged), not a DELETE-heavy
#     writer.
# `tests/test_gate7_sparse_units_001.py` holds this as an explicit accepted-
# overlap list and fails if any OTHER calendar unit starts covering :37 — and it
# also fails if an accepted overlap stops being named here.
# IF THIS RESIDUAL IS EVER JUDGED TOO EXPENSIVE, the fix is one constant:
# `SPARSE_TIMER_PHASE_MINUTE = 47` clears the backup window entirely and every
# other calendar neighbour, at no cost to invariant (2). It was not taken here
# because :37 is the approved value and this residual was found after.
#
# WHY THE COLLISION WAS WORTH MOVING RATHER THAN PRICING. The direct contention
# cost really is small — this lane's write phase is 25-token batches with <= 50
# rows per commit and a MEASURED max competing-writer stall of 45 ms IN THAT
# WRITE PHASE (the source measurement, B7 in
# tests/test_crypto_coverage_repair_002.py, put the FETCH phase at 28 ms and the
# PRELUDE at 2,023 ms — 45 ms is the smallest of the three, and it is quoted
# because the write phase is what a grid collision contends with). What made :00
# different from an ordinary collision is that THIS TIMER ARRIVES FIRST,
# DETERMINISTICALLY, AND CANNOT BE MOVED OUT OF THE WAY BY JITTER. It is the
# only timer in this directory with `RandomizedDelaySec=0` AND `AccuracySec=1s`
# — see the zero-jitter derivation below, which is forced by invariant (2) and
# leaves nothing to spend — so at every :00 stampede it sat at the head of a
# window it could not be jittered out of. The phase is the ONE knob that moves
# the lattice at zero cost to invariant (2): containment of a closed 2*BAND
# interval by a CADENCE-spaced lattice is translation-invariant, so the gap, and
# therefore the invariant, is untouched. Pinned by
# `test_the_phase_offset_costs_invariant_two_nothing`.
#
# WHAT :37 DOES NOT BUY. probability-arena-marketops.timer and
# probability-arena-meme-news.timer are BOOT-anchored (`OnUnitActiveSec=`), so
# their grids drift with uptime and NO fixed phase can dodge them permanently.
# That collision remains priced, not avoided, at the tens-of-milliseconds
# write-phase figure above.
OnCalendar=*-*-* *:37:00
# ZERO JITTER, AND THIS IS DERIVED, NOT A PREFERENCE. Invariant (2) needs at
# least 2 scheduled passes inside every closed band of length 2*BAND = 120 min.
# At the shipped pair (BAND 60, CADENCE 60) that holds with EXACTLY ZERO SLACK:
# a lattice of gap g puts at least 2 points in a 120-minute closed interval only
# while g <= 60 min. Every second of scheduler jitter is therefore spent from a
# budget that is already empty, and a single 61-minute gap drops a band to one
# scheduled pass — the state invariant (2) exists to prevent.
#   * RandomizedDelaySec is the jitter that is OURS to remove, so it is 0. The
#     reconciler's 300 s would be a straight invariant violation here.
#   * AccuracySec cannot be removed, only shrunk: systemd's default of 1 min
#     coalesces timers into a 60 s window, which is 1.7% of this cadence and on
#     its own enough to produce a 61-minute gap. 1 s is 0.03%.
# The containment argument is exact at zero jitter and degrades continuously; 1s
# is the smallest honest claim, not a guarantee of a perfect lattice.
RandomizedDelaySec=0
AccuracySec=1s
# Persistent=false, and the trade is real rather than free. A catch-up firing
# can only SHORTEN a gap, so it could not break invariant (2) and would
# occasionally rescue a band that closes during an outage. It is declined
# anyway: a catch-up lands in the boot storm alongside marketops
# OnBootSec=2min, meme-news 3min and tick-aggregation 5min with the watcher
# already writing, and this lane is a PROVIDER lane — it would spend real
# requests, at boot, against a MarketOps health read taken before MarketOps has
# run. THE COST OF THIS CHOICE, stated plainly: invariant (2) tolerates exactly
# ONE missed pass, and with no catch-up a second consecutive miss is a
# permanent, never-backfilled `scheduling_miss` for every band that closes in
# between.
Persistent=false
Unit=probability-arena-crypto-sparse-observe.service

[Install]
WantedBy=timers.target
